Computing $2^{81} \bmod 125$: $2^{40} \equiv 26$, $2^{80} \equiv 26^2 = 676 \equiv 51$, $2^{81} \equiv 102 \pmod{125}$.

So $p+q \equiv 8 + 102 - 2 = 108 \pmod{125}$.

**CRT:** Find $n$ with $n \equiv 108 \pmod{125}$ and $n \equiv 6 \pmod{8}$.

$n = 108 + 125k$. Since $108 \equiv 4 \pmod{8}$ and $125 \equiv 5 \pmod{8}$: $4 + 5k \equiv 6 \pmod{8}$, so $5k \equiv 2$, giving $k \equiv 2 \pmod{8}$.

$$n = 108 + 250 = 358$$

